## Keep all five distribution tiles visible at every width

## Context

Reported as "the distribution charts can be hidden if the page width is too
small". Rendering the strip in headless Chrome at a range of widths found two
separate causes, only one of which was the reported symptom.

The real one affected *wide* windows. `grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr)`
means `minmax(auto, 1fr)`, and a canvas has a 300px intrinsic width, so every
track had a 300px floor. Five tracks plus gaps demanded ~1530px, and any
container narrower than that overflowed — clipping Clustering, and then
Closeness, off the right edge. At a 1400px viewport only four tiles were
reachable.

Below 1200px a second rule dropped the grid to two columns, wrapping five
tiles into three ragged rows (2 + 2 + 1, the last one stretched full width)
and pushing the strip below the fold.

## Changes

1. `src/front/static/query/css/query-analytics.css` — the five-column track is
   now `minmax(0, 1fr)`, so tracks shrink below the canvas's intrinsic width
   and Chart.js resizes into them. Verified at 1250px, 1400px and 1600px: all
   five tiles fit and are evenly sized.
2. Same file — between 577px and 1200px the strip is a six-column grid with
   the first two tiles spanning three columns and the last three spanning two,
   giving two balanced rows (PageRank + betweenness, then degree, closeness,
   clustering) instead of the ragged wrap. Six divides evenly by both 2 and 3.
   This relies on the strip holding exactly the five `_ALL_METRICS` tiles,
   which it always does — the per-tile empty state is rendered inside the tile.
3. Same file — kept the single-column fallback below 577px. The 2+3 split was
   tested there and leaves ~165px per tile on the second row, which wraps the
   caption and leaves the two rows unequal heights.
4. `tests/units/front/test_analytics_dashboard.py` — added
   `TestAllFiveTilesStayVisible` (6 tests) and a `css` fixture. Each test was
   verified to fail when the rule it pins is reverted, including a mutation
   that swaps the row spans and one that strips the comment explaining why
   `minmax(0, 1fr)` cannot be simplified back to `1fr`.

No markup or JS change: the tile order already matched the requested grouping.

