# Stop reporting an unreachable graph engine as "Graph not built"

## Context

On a Neo4j (Aura) project the Build page listed the graph as present while every
KG sub-page — Analytics included — showed the "Graph not built" badge and a "Go
to Build" button, inviting a rebuild of a graph that was already there.

The two pages answer the same question through different plumbing. The badge
reads `/dtwin/sync/info` → `triplestore_status.has_data`, which `sync_info`
serves cache-first from the domain session (`_TS_STATS_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS`, 5
min). The Build page's artefact cards read `/dtwin/sync/dt-existence`, which is
fetched with `force_refresh` and so always re-probes.

Two defects turned that into a contradiction:

1. `fetch_graph_triplestore_status` swallowed every exception from
   `table_exists`/`get_status` and returned a confident `has_data=False` with
   `reason="Graph does not exist yet"`. A timeout was indistinguishable from a
   graph that had never been built.
2. `get_or_fetch_graph_status` then cached that unconditionally, with no way to
   force a re-check — so one slow call poisoned the badge for five minutes. A
   `SHOW CONSTRAINTS` against Aura was observed taking 958 ms, which is exactly
   the latency profile that triggers it.

`get_or_fetch_dt_existence` already guards against this and says so in its
docstring — "caching a transient failure as if it were a confirmed absence is
the bug this whole pathway exists to prevent". The guard had never been extended
to the `status` section.

Compounding it, the live probe's answer never reached the badge: `_loadDtExistence`
applied the force-refreshed result to the Build page's cards only, and
`loadSyncInfo` actively overrode a live `graph_has_data=true` to false on any
falsy status, on the stated assumption that `triplestore_status` was "a more
targeted, recent check" — but it is the cached one.

## Changes

1. `src/back/objects/digitaltwin/DigitalTwin.py` —
   `fetch_graph_triplestore_status` now returns a tri-state `has_data`:
   `True`/`False` once the probe answers, `None` when the engine could not be
   reached, with the cause in a new `graph_check_error` field. The inconclusive
   case no longer claims the graph does not exist.
2. Same file — `reason` for a definite negative is keyed on existence rather
   than the row count, so an existing-but-empty graph reads "Graph is empty"
   instead of "Graph does not exist yet".
3. Same file — `get_or_fetch_graph_status` never caches an inconclusive result
   and takes a `force_refresh` flag, mirroring `get_or_fetch_dt_existence`.
4. `src/api/routers/internal/dtwin.py` — `/dtwin/sync/status` wires its
   long-ignored `refresh` query param to `force_refresh`. The frontend already
   documented that flag as forcing a live check, so this makes the Build page's
   Refresh button the escape hatch from a stale entry.
5. `src/front/static/query/js/query-sync.js` — added `tripleStoreStatusUnknown`
   and a distinct amber "Status unavailable" badge state, reached before the
   not-built branch and deliberately without a "Go to Build" button.
6. Same file — new `_reconcileReadinessWithLiveProbe`, called from
   `_loadDtExistence`, promotes readiness and repaints the indicators when the
   force-refreshed probe finds graph data the cached status missed. Only an
   affirmative live result promotes; a pending skeleton or unknown is ignored.
7. Same file — the cache-staleness override now fires only on a definite
   `has_data === false`, so a failed probe can no longer erase what the live
   existence check found. A failed status *request* is now treated as unknown
   rather than as an absent graph.
8. Same file — `renderTripleStoreStatus` gained an unknown branch. An
   inconclusive probe used to fall through to "Graph is empty. Run
   Synchronize", which is a claim the probe never supported; it now reports the
   connection error.
9. `src/front/static/query/js/query-databricks-build.js` — the Delta build
   status text distinguishes an unreachable engine ("Status unavailable") from
   an empty graph ("No data yet").
10. `tests/units/dtwin/test_graph_status_probe_failure.py` — new file, 15 tests
   covering the tri-state probe, the never-cache-an-unknown contract,
   `force_refresh`, and guard tests pinning the premise (a confident answer is
   still cached; the TTL is long enough to matter). All 9 behavioural tests were
   confirmed red before the fix.
11. `tests/units/front/test_kg_readiness_indicator.py` — added
   `TestAnUnreachableEngineIsNotAMissingGraph` and
   `TestTheLiveProbeCorrectsTheCachedBadge` (13 tests). Six mutations were run
   and all caught, including neutering the unknown branch with an added
   `false &&`, reverting the override condition, dropping the reconcile call,
   dropping the pending guard, and removing the repaint. The first pass of the
   ordering test missed the `false &&` mutation, so it now pins the whole branch
   condition rather than just the identifier's position.

